# Cursor Rules for Ezkey Project

## Java Code Formatting Standards

### Indentation and Spacing
- Strictly apply google style formatting rules

### Naming Conventions
- Classes: PascalCase (e.g., `IntegrationController`)
- Methods and variables: camelCase (e.g., `getIntegrationById`)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`)
- Packages: lowercase (e.g., `org.ezkey.integration`)

### Import Organization
Organize imports in this order:
1. Java core imports
2. Third-party library imports
3. Spring framework imports
4. Project-specific imports

Example:
```java
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.List;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

import org.ezkey.integration.domain.entity.EzkeyIntegration;
```

### Javadoc Standards
- Always include `@since 2025` for new classes
- Include project header with copyright and license
- Document all public methods with `@param`, `@return`, and `@throws` where applicable
- Use descriptive comments that explain "why" not "what"

### Code Structure
- Follow the established package structure:
  - `domain.entity` - JPA entities
  - `domain.repository` - Spring Data repositories
  - `service` - Business logic
  - `controller` - REST endpoints
  - `dto.request` - Request DTOs
  - `dto.response` - Response DTOs
  - `dto.common` - Shared DTOs
  - `mapper` - MapStruct mappers
  - `exception` - Custom exceptions

### REST API Standards
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes (200, 201, 404, 500)
- Return 201 Created with Location header for POST operations
- Use consistent URL patterns: `/api/v1/{resource}`
- Always return DTOs, never expose entities directly

### Error Handling
- Use `ResourceNotFoundException` for 404 scenarios
- Implement global exception handling with `@RestControllerAdvice`
- Return standardized error responses with code, message, and timestamp

### Dependencies and Imports
- Use MapStruct for object mapping
- Use Spring Data JPA for persistence
- Prefer constructor injection over field injection
- Use `@Autowired` annotation for clarity

### Testing Standards
- Write unit tests for services
- Write integration tests for controllers
- Use descriptive test method names
- Follow AAA pattern (Arrange, Act, Assert)

### Git Commit Standards
- Use conventional commit messages
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Include issue numbers when applicable

## File Organization
- One public class per file
- File name matches class name exactly
- Group related functionality in packages
- Keep packages focused and cohesive

## Performance Considerations
- Use lazy loading for JPA relationships when appropriate
- Implement proper pagination for large datasets
- Use appropriate fetch strategies
- Consider caching for frequently accessed data

## Security Guidelines
- Validate all input data
- Use parameterized queries (handled by JPA)
- Implement proper authentication and authorization
- Sanitize user inputs
- Use HTTPS in production

## Documentation Requirements
- Maintain up-to-date README.md
- Document API endpoints with OpenAPI/Swagger
- Include setup and deployment instructions
- Document configuration options 

# Encodage UTF-8 sans BOM - Règle critique

## Génération de code
- TOUJOURS générer du code en UTF-8 sans BOM
- JAMAIS utiliser d'encodage UTF-16 ou UTF-8 avec BOM
- Vérifier que tous les fichiers Java générés sont en UTF-8 pur
- Éviter tout caractère invisible ou BOM dans les générations

## Validation obligatoire
Avant de générer un fichier, confirmer mentalement : "Ce fichier sera en UTF-8 sans BOM"

## Impact
Les fichiers UTF-16 ou avec BOM causent des erreurs de compilation Maven et des problèmes d'encodage.

# Line Endings - Unix Style (LF only)

## File Generation
- ALWAYS use Unix-style line endings (LF, `\n`) when generating or writing files
- NEVER use Windows-style line endings (CRLF, `\r\n`)
- This applies to ALL file types: Java, SQL, Markdown, properties, JSON, XML, etc.

## Validation
Before generating a file, confirm: "This file will use LF line endings only"

## Impact
Consistent LF line endings ensure:
- Cross-platform compatibility (Linux, macOS, Windows)
- Git diff clarity (no false positives from line ending changes)
- Consistent behavior across development environments
- Avoids issues with scripts and tools that expect LF

# Terminal Command Optimization Rules

## Command Execution Best Practices
- AVOID long compound commands with `&&` or `;` that can block
- PREFER short, focused commands executed separately
- USE `cd` first, then execute the main command in a separate call
- AVOID `mvn clean compile` - prefer `mvn compile` for faster execution
- USE `mvn compile` instead of `mvn clean compile` when possible

## Maven Optimization
- When possible, use `mvn compile` instead of `mvn clean compile`
- For quick syntax checks, prefer reading files directly over compilation
- Use `mvn help:effective-settings` sparingly as it can be slow
- Consider using `mvn validate` for quick project validation

## Alternative to Compilation
- READ source files directly to verify syntax and structure
- CHECK imports and dependencies by examining file content
- VALIDATE Thymeleaf templates by reading HTML structure
- USE code analysis tools instead of full compilation when possible

## Command Structure
- BREAK complex commands into simple steps
- EXECUTE directory changes separately from build commands
- USE PowerShell syntax correctly: `& "path/to/executable"` for external tools
- AVOID bash-specific syntax when using PowerShell

## Performance Monitoring
- If a command blocks for more than 30 seconds, consider alternatives
- Use file reading and analysis instead of compilation when appropriate
- Prefer direct code review over automated compilation checks
